# Line endings.
#
# Without this file, a Windows checkout gets CRLF in every text file, because
# core.autocrlf=true is what the Git for Windows installer sets by default and
# there was nothing here to override it. For most projects that is cosmetic. For
# this one it is fatal, and fatal in the worst way -- `git status` stays clean,
# because git normalizes the CRs back out on the way into the index, so the
# breakage exists only in the working tree and is invisible to every git command
# anyone would think to run.
#
# What it breaks:
#
#   src/Makefile   GNU make takes the CR as part of the value. X86_ISA becomes
#                  "x86-64-v3\r" and the -march probe fails, silently costing the
#                  2x that Windows builds encounter; BC_WINDOWS becomes "1\r" and
#                  compares unequal to "1", switching off every Windows branch;
#                  and each recipe line reaches sh with a trailing CR, which
#                  reports as the memorable `$'\r': command not found`.
#   *.sh           Same, plus a CR on the shebang line makes the kernel look for
#                  an interpreter named "/bin/sh\r" and report "no such file or
#                  directory" while naming a file that plainly exists.
#
# C sources, Python and JSON tolerate CRLF, so normalizing them is about keeping
# the tree in one state rather than about anything failing.
#
# eol=lf, not just text=auto: text=auto alone would still hand Windows CRLF under
# autocrlf=true, which is precisely the configuration this is written for.
# Unix checkouts are unaffected -- they were already LF both in the index and on
# disk, so this changes nothing there.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit rather than relying on git's content heuristic. These are the model
# payloads and the test fixtures; a stray byte rewrite in any of them would not
# fail the build, it would change the segmentation, which `make test` would then
# report as a numeric regression with no hint that the file had been mangled in
# transit. The heuristic is good and would almost certainly catch all of them,
# but "almost certainly" is the wrong standard for the weights.
*.fatbin        binary
*.bin           binary
*.safetensors   binary
*.pth           binary
*.nii           binary
*.nii.gz        binary
*.gz            binary
*.png           binary
*.pkg           binary
